.TH tbcx 3 "Aug 7, 2026" "tbcx 1.2.1" "Tcl Bytecode eXchange"
.SH NAME
tbcx \- serialize, load, and inspect precompiled Tcl 9.1 bytecode (procs, OO methods, and lambdas). Artifacts require an exact Tcl major/minor/patch/release-type match at load time.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBtbcx::save\fR \fIin out\fR ?\fB\-include\-source\fR?
\fBtbcx::load\fR \fIin\fR
\fBtbcx::dump\fR \fIfilename\fR
\fBtbcx::gc\fR
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtbcx\fR extension provides four commands that enable an efficient
\fIsave \[->] load \[->] eval\fR pipeline for Tcl 9.1 scripts. The goal is to pay the cost of
parsing/compiling at save time so that loading is as fast as reading a compact binary, while
remaining functionally equivalent to \fBsource\fR of the original script.
.PP
Artifacts store the compiled top\-level, precompiled \fBproc\fR bodies, TclOO method/ctor/dtor
bodies, and \fBlambda\fR literals for use with \fBapply\fR. Loading installs these into the current
interpreter and executes the top\-level block in the caller's current namespace, with source\-equivalent
semantics for variable scope, frame identity, and \fBinfo script\fR.
.PP
In safe interpreters, \fBtbcx_SafeInit\fR provides the package and type
infrastructure but does \fBnot\fR register any \fBtbcx::*\fR commands.
A parent interpreter may selectively grant access with
\fBinterp alias\fR or \fBinterp expose\fR.

.SH COMMANDS
.SS "tbcx::save in out ?-include-source?"
.B Synopsis
.PP
Compile a script and write a \fB.tbcx\fR artifact.
.PP
.B Parameters
.TP
.I in
Resolved in this order:
.RS
.IP "1." 3
\fIOpen channel name\fR \- if the value names an existing open channel, it is read as text (the caller controls encoding; TBCX does not alter it).
.IP "2." 3
\fIReadable file path\fR \- if the value is a path to a readable file, it is opened in text mode (default UTF\-8), read, and closed by \fBtbcx::save\fR. The normalized path is recorded in the artifact header for \fBinfo script\fR restoration at load time.
.IP "3." 3
\fILiteral script text\fR \- otherwise the value is treated as inline Tcl script text. Consequently, a value that looks like a path but is not currently readable is compiled as script text, not reported as a file\-open error. No source path is recorded.
.RE
.TP
.I out
One of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fIWritable channel\fR \- an open channel; binary mode (\fC\-translation binary \-eofchar {}\fR) is enforced. The caller's channel settings are mutated and \fInot\fR restored. The channel is \fInot\fR closed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIWritable path\fR \- a filesystem path; TBCX writes a temporary file in the target directory and renames it into place only after serialization succeeds, so a failed save never leaves a truncated artifact at the final path.
.RE
.TP
.B \-include\-source
Optional flag.  Selects include\-source policy and embeds the exact authored
bytes in every executable\-source field: \fBproc\fR, TclOO method,
\fBTBCX_LIT_BYTESRC\fR, and lambda body.  Required when consumers depend on \fBinfo body\fR,
\fBinfo class definition\fR, TIP #280 line attribution in stack traces, disassembly
annotations, or introspection\-based clone idioms (e.g.
\fCproc $new [info args $src] [info body $src]\fR) to return the original
authored text.  The retained text is introspection data only; execution still
uses precompiled bytecode and never falls back to these fields.
.PP
\fBDefault behavior (no \-include\-source):\fR The top block selects strict
compiled\-only policy. Every proc, method, BYTESRC, and lambda executable\-source
field is an empty LPString. Proc and method introspection receives the diagnostic
sentinel described in \fBSOURCE PRESERVATION\fR below; stripped lambdas receive a
separate fixed list\-shaped diagnostic representation. Ordinary Tcl literals remain
in bytecode, so this policy removes designated complete source fields but is not encryption.
.PP
.B Behavior
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Performs a single\-pass capture and rewrite of the script, extracting \fBproc\fR, \fBnamespace eval\fR,
\fBoo::class create\fR, \fBoo::define\fR/\fBoo::objdefine\fR, and \fBself method\fR definitions while
producing a rewritten script with method bodies replaced by recognizable stub sentinels.
Each captured method's TclOO visibility is recorded in a per\-record scope byte
(default / public / unexported / TIP #500 true\-private), derived from the
\fB\-export\fR/\fB\-unexport\fR/\fB\-private\fR definition options, lexical
\fBprivate { ... }\fR and \fBself { ... }\fR context, and same\-body
\fBexport\fR/\fBunexport\fR/\fBself export\fR/\fBself unexport\fR commands.
An origin byte distinguishes class\-definition methods (\fBoo::define\fR /
\fBoo::class create\fR) from per\-object methods (\fBoo::objdefine\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Compiles the top\-level script without borrowing the saver caller's procedure-local
slot table: the compile environment's proc pointer is cleared and the caller frame's
LocalCache is hidden for the duration of compilation.  At load time the block still
executes in the load caller's current namespace and frame.
.IP \(bu 2
Rejects, with a diagnostic, a \fBself method\fR (or a method inside a
\fBself { ... }\fR block) written in an \fBoo::objdefine\fR builder \(em
\fBoo::objdefine\fR has no \fBself method\fR form, so such a definition cannot be
reconstructed and would otherwise load as a silent no\-op.
.IP \(bu 2
Pre\-compiles namespace eval body literals and other script\-body patterns (\fBtry\fR/\fBon\fR/\fBtrap\fR/\fBfinally\fR, \fBforeach\fR, \fBlmap\fR, \fBwhile\fR, \fBfor\fR, \fBcatch\fR, \fBif\fR/\fBelseif\fR/\fBelse\fR, \fBeval\fR, \fBuplevel\fR, \fBtime\fR, \fBtimerate\fR, \fBdict for\fR/\fBmap\fR/\fBupdate\fR/\fBwith\fR, \fBlsort \-command\fR, and \fBself method\fR bodies inside \fBoo::define\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Builds an occurrence\-level executable literal plan from the generic
\fBinvokeStk\fR stream.  The stack model identifies exact \fBpush\fR
instructions rather than globally classifying a literal object or its bytes.
Repeated equal script bodies therefore remain separate compiled records, while
an equal occurrence used as data remains an ordinary literal.
.IP \(bu 2
Compiles every new\-policy top\-level, proc, method, lambda, and nested script
block with \fBDONT_COMPILE_CMDS_INLINE\fR and materializes it as
\fBTCL_BYTECODE_PRECOMPILED\fR. Command implementation changes are handled by
generic dispatch, without source recompilation.
.IP \(bu 2
Detects bytearray literals (bytes \[>=] 0x80) and emits them as \fBTBCX_LIT_BYTEARR\fR to prevent UTF\-8 corruption.
.IP \(bu 2
Body literals are emitted as \fBTBCX_LIT_BYTESRC\fR containing namespace-bound
compiled bytecode and a policy-controlled source field. Known literal crossings
through \fBinterp eval\fR are currently rejected with
\fBTBCX EVAL CROSSINTERP UNSUPPORTED\fR; no source fallback is used.
.IP \(bu 2
When the input is a readable file path, the normalized path is recorded in the header via \fBTcl_FSGetNormalizedPath\fR so the loader can restore \fBinfo script\fR to the authored value.
.IP \(bu 2
Serializes the header, top\-level block, Procs table (with per\-proc source\-text LPString), Classes catalog (advisory, alphabetically sorted for deterministic output), and Methods table in definition order (with per\-method scope byte, origin byte, source\-text LPString, and kind 4 for self methods; ctor/dtor records always carry the default scope).
.IP \(bu 2
Lambda literals (\fBapply\fR forms) are compiled and serialized as lambda\-bytecode literals.
.IP \(bu 2
Conflicting proc definitions across if/else branches are handled via indexed markers, enabling position\-based matching at load time.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns
.RS
The output object: either the normalized path written, or the writable channel handle.
.RE
.PP
.B Errors
.RS
Typical errors include: read error from the input channel or opened file; unwritable output; unknown/unsupported AuxData;
size limit exceeded (code, literals, AuxData, exception ranges, strings); runaway serialization
(too many literals, blocks, excessive recursion depth, or output too large);
an unsupported statically recognized cross-interpreter script occurrence;
or short read/write.
The interpreter result contains a descriptive message.
.RE
.PP
.B Notes
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Input channels retain their encoding settings; output channels are set to binary mode.
.IP \(bu 2
The produced artifact targets Tcl 9.1 (format version 93); other versions are rejected at load time.
.RE
.PP
.B Examples
.nf
# Save from path \-> path (default: source stripped)
set path [tbcx::save ./app.tcl ./app.tbcx]

# Save from path \-> path with body source preserved for info body /
# info class definition / TIP #280 line attribution.
tbcx::save ./app.tcl ./app.tbcx -include-source

# Save from string value \-> path
set script {proc hi {} {puts Hello}; hi}
tbcx::save $script ./hello.tbcx

# Save from channel \-> channel
set in  [open ./lib/foo.tcl r]
set out [open ./foo.tbcx w]
fconfigure $out -translation binary -eofchar {}
try {
    tbcx::save $in $out
} finally {
    close $in
    close $out
}
.fi

.SS "tbcx::load in"
.B Synopsis
.PP
Load a \fB.tbcx\fR artifact, install precompiled entities, and execute the top\-level block in the caller's current namespace.
.PP
.B Parameters
.TP
.I in
One of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fIReadable channel\fR \- an open channel positioned at the beginning of a \fB.tbcx\fR stream (binary).
.IP \(bu 2
\fIReadable path\fR \- a filesystem path to a \fB.tbcx\fR file.
.RE
.PP
.B Behavior
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Reads the bounded artifact into an owned, interpreter-neutral byte image and
validates the header and exact packed producer version (major, minor, patch, and
alpha/beta/final release type).
.IP \(bu 2
Completes a preflight pass over every section before constructing any namespace,
Tcl object, ByteCode, Proc, method, or persistent shim. The pass validates source
policy and all executable-source fields, opcodes and instruction boundaries,
operands, literal/local/AuxData indices, jump targets, exception metadata,
supported AuxData payloads, nested policy words, and exact end-of-file.
.IP \(bu 2
If the header carries a recorded authored source path, reads it into a Tcl_Obj for \fBinfo script\fR restoration.
.IP \(bu 2
Installs a temporary \fBProcShim\fR that intercepts the \fBproc\fR command at the
authored definition marker.  When the name and argument signature match, it passes
the already-deserialized procbody to Tcl's original \fBproc\fR handler.  Tcl therefore
performs normal marker-time creation, replacement traces, namespace/export/import
handling, and argument validation while reusing the serialized Proc and compiled-local
chain.  The body's string representation is set to either the preserved source text
(artifacts built with \fB\-include\-source\fR) or the diagnostic sentinel (default
stripped mode).
.IP \(bu 2
Installs a temporary \fBOOShim\fR that intercepts \fBoo::define\fR and \fBoo::objdefine\fR
by patching the commands' handler pointers in place (the commands are \fInot\fR renamed)
to substitute precompiled method/constructor/destructor bodies.  Method records are keyed
by \fI(class FQN, kind, name, origin)\fR and held in definition\-order FIFOs; each
definition site the rewritten script executes consumes the front record, and only bodies
carrying the tbcx stub sentinel are patched \(em a method the saver left verbatim is never
overwritten by a stale same\-key record.  After installing each body, the loader applies
the record's visibility scope (public, unexported, or TIP #500 true\-private).
Self methods (kind 4, \fBTBCX_METH_SELF\fR) are installed via \fBoo::define\fR with a
\fBself method\fR builder body to preserve metaclass inheritance for subclasses.
Per\-object methods are installed precompiled by replaying the \fBoo::objdefine\fR builder
body against object\-origin records; true\-private per\-object methods are re\-created via
the flat \fBprivate\fR form.  Method body string representations follow the same
preserved\-or\-sentinel rule as proc bodies.
.IP \(bu 2
The shims protect themselves with command traces on \fBproc\fR, \fBoo::define\fR,
\fBoo::objdefine\fR, and \fBapply\fR: if any of these is renamed or deleted while a shim
is active, the trace restores the original handlers in place immediately, so a renamed
command never remains wired to freed shim state.
.IP \(bu 2
Registers precompiled lambdas in a persistent per\-interpreter \fBApplyShim\fR that
recovers the compiled representation if type shimmer evicts it.  A later load
reactivates the shim if \fBapply\fR was renamed, deleted, and restored or recreated.
.IP \(bu 2
Executes the precompiled top\-level block via \fBTcl_EvalObjEx\fR with flags \fB0\fR
(no \fBTCL_EVAL_GLOBAL\fR), evaluating in the caller's current namespace.
\fBiPtr->scriptFile\fR is saved, set to the header's source path (or the tbcx
artifact path as a fallback when loading from a file), and restored after
evaluation \(em matching \fBTcl_FSEvalFileEx\fR's handling in
\fBtclIOUtil.c\fR.  When loading from an already\-open channel and the header
carries no recorded path, \fBinfo script\fR is left untouched.
The normal zero-local top-level leaves every field of the caller's active frame
untouched.  If a block carries named local slots, an overlay is resolved against
the original caller frame before installation and the exact frame state is restored
after every return code.  A top\-level \fBreturn\fR is handled identically to \fBsource\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Removes the ProcShim and OOShim after execution; the ApplyShim persists for the interpreter's lifetime.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns
.RS
The result of the top\-level block evaluation.
.RE
.PP
.B Errors
.RS
Typical errors include: unreadable input; bad header; incompatible Tcl version; malformed/unknown section;
size limit exceeded; short read; class or namespace creation errors during top\-level evaluation.
.RE
.PP
.B Notes
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The top\-level block executes in the \fIcaller's\fR current namespace, with
compiled locals linked to the caller's variable frame.  When \fBtbcx::load\fR
is wrapped inside a proc that the user invokes externally, the caller should
use \fBuplevel 1 [list tbcx::load $path]\fR to pop to the outer frame \(em
identical to the pattern already required for \fBsource\fR in the same position.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinfo script\fR inside the loaded top\-level block returns the authored
\fB.tcl\fR source path when the artifact was built from a file, matching what
\fBsource\fR would have set.  This supports both \fB[file dirname [info script]]\fR
(for sibling asset lookup) and \fB[info script] eq $::argv0\fR (self\-invocation guards).
.IP \(bu 2
Loading executes code; only load artifacts from trusted sources.
.RE
.PP
.B Examples
.nf
# Load into current interp
tbcx::load ./app.tbcx

# Load from an open channel
set ch [open ./app.tbcx r]
fconfigure $ch -translation binary -eofchar {}
try {
    tbcx::load $ch
} finally {
    close $ch
}

# Drop-in replacement for source, inside a module loader proc.
# Both branches use uplevel 1 so they reach the caller's frame.
proc moduleLoad {path} {
    set tbcxPath [file join [file dirname $path] ../tbcx [file tail $path].tbcx]
    if {[file exists $tbcxPath]} {
        uplevel 1 [list tbcx::load $tbcxPath]
    } else {
        uplevel 1 [list source $path]
    }
}
.fi

.SS "tbcx::dump filename"
.B Synopsis
.PP
Disassemble and describe a \fB.tbcx\fR artifact in human\-readable form.
.PP
.B Parameters
.TP
.I filename
A readable path to a \fB.tbcx\fR file.
.PP
.B Behavior
.RS
Validates the artifact through the same complete preflight path as
\fBtbcx::load\fR and prints: header fields (including the authored source path
and \fBsourcePolicy\fR); executable-source field, nonempty-field, and byte totals;
section summaries; literals and AuxData; exception ranges;
disassembly of the top\-level, proc, method, and lambda bodies; and the
preserved body source text (indented inline, no truncation) for each proc and
method when the artifact was built with \fB\-include\-source\fR.  For stripped
artifacts (the default), each body record shows \fBsource: <stripped at save time>\fR.
An exact empty body retained by \fB\-include\-source\fR is shown as
\fBsource: (0 bytes)\fR, not as stripped.
Each method record also shows its visibility \fBscope\fR and \fBorigin\fR
(class\-definition vs object\-definition).  Literal operands in the disassembly
are annotated using the instruction table's operand types rather than
instruction\-name heuristics.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns
.RS
A string containing the formatted dump.
.RE
.PP
.B Errors
.RS
Unreadable file; bad header; malformed section; short read.
.RE
.PP
.B Examples
.nf
% package require tbcx
% tbcx::save hello.tcl hello.tbcx -include-source
% puts [tbcx::dump hello.tbcx]
TBCX Header:
  magic = 0x58434254 ('T''B''C''X')
  format = 93
  tcl_version = 9.1.0 (type 0)
  top: code=12, except=0, lits=2, aux=0, locals=1, stack=2
  source = /path/to/hello.tcl

Top-level block:
  Disassembly (top-level):
    ...

Procs: 1
  - proc hi  (ns=::)
    args:
    source: (14 bytes)
      puts Hello
    Disassembly (hi):
    ...
.fi

.SS "tbcx::gc"
.B Synopsis
.PP
Purge stale entries from the per\-interpreter lambda shimmer\-recovery registry.
.PP
.B Behavior
.RS
The \fBApplyShim\fR maintains a registry of precompiled lambda objects. Over time,
entries for lambdas that are no longer referenced (except by the registry itself)
accumulate. This command purges those stale entries immediately.
.PP
Stale entries are also purged lazily on each \fBtbcx::load\fR call, so explicit
use of \fBtbcx::gc\fR is typically unnecessary except in long\-running interpreters
that load many \fB.tbcx\fR files and want to reclaim memory sooner.
.PP
\fBtbcx::gc\fR is a no\-op if no ApplyShim has been installed yet (i.e. before
any \fBtbcx::load\fR call), and it is safe to call multiple times.
.RE
.PP
.B Parameters
.RS
None.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns
.RS
Empty string.
.RE

.SH SOURCE PRESERVATION
.PP
Without \fB\-include\-source\fR, every designated executable\-source field
(proc, method/constructor/destructor, BYTESRC, and lambda body) is empty on
the wire. Ordinary Tcl literals remain in bytecode, so this is not encryption.
For proc and method bodies the loader installs this two\-line diagnostic sentinel:
.PP
.nf
    # tbcx: body source stripped at save time; info body unavailable
    error "tbcx: introspection-based cloning is not supported for this artifact"
.fi
.PP
The first line is a Tcl comment visible to introspection and traces. The second
line raises an error if code copies and evaluates the diagnostic body.
.PP
Stripped lambda records use a separate fixed list\-shaped diagnostic string.
Their registered \fBlambdaExpr\fR representation still holds the materialized
Proc. A newly constructed object containing only the diagnostic has lost TBCX
identity and fails instead of compiling an apparent authored body.
.PP
With \fB\-include\-source\fR, exact authored bytes are preserved in all four
executable\-source field kinds and attached for introspection as appropriate.
The ByteCode internal representation is untouched and PRECOMPILED; execution
still uses compiled material even if retained bytes are changed. \fBinfo body\fR,
\fBinfo class definition\fR, \fBinfo class constructor\fR, and TIP #280 source
attribution round\-trip byte\-for\-byte.

.SH FILE FORMAT (OVERVIEW)
.PP
This section summarizes the on\-disk structure. Format version remains 93 (Tcl 9.1).
All integers are little\-endian.
.TP
.B Header
Magic (0x58434254) + format version (93) + producing Tcl version; size/count metadata for the top\-level block
(code length, exception ranges, literal count, AuxData count, locals, max stack); authored source path LPString
(empty for inline/channel inputs).
.TP
.B Sections (order)
(1) Top\-level block (code, literals, AuxData, exceptions, locals epilogue,
including the source\-policy u32; every nested block keeps that field zero);
(2) Procs (FQN, ns, arg spec, body\-source LPString, compiled block);
(3) Classes (advisory catalog of discovered class names; actual creation occurs at load time via the top\-level script);
(4) Methods, in definition order (class FQN; kind u8: 0=inst, 1=class, 2=ctor, 3=dtor, 4=self; scope u8: 0=default, 1=public, 2=unexported, 3=TIP #500 true\-private \(em ctor/dtor always 0; origin u8: 0=class definition, 1=object definition; name \(em empty for ctor/dtor; args; body\-source LPString; compiled block).  Records are keyed at load by \fI(class FQN, kind, name, origin)\fR, so a class\-instance method and a per\-object method sharing a name coexist.
.TP
.B Literal kinds
Tagged u32 values: 0 bignum (sign u8, magnitude length u32, little\-endian
magnitude bytes), 1 boolean (one byte), 2 bytearray (length + raw bytes),
3 dict (pair count, then key/value literal pairs; insertion order preserved),
4 double (IEEE\-754 bits as u64), 5 list (element count, then nested
literals), 6 string (LPString), 7 wideint (signed 64\-bit as u64),
8 wideuint (unsigned 64\-bit), 9 lambda\-bytecode (namespace FQN, arguments,
compiled block, policy\-controlled body source text) for use with \fBapply\fR,
and 10 bytesrc (policy\-controlled source text, namespace FQN, compiled block).
.TP
.B Source policy
Top policy 0 permits source\-backed execution, 1 is strict compiled\-only,
and 2 is include\-source. Saves write 1 by default or 2 with
\fB\-include\-source\fR. Both nonzero policies execute PRECOMPILED material,
and policy\-zero format\-93 artifacts remain readable.
.TP
.B AuxData families
jump tables (string or numeric), dictupdate, and NewForeachInfo.

.SH LAMBDA SUPPORT
.PP
Literal arguments in statically recognized \fBapply\fR occurrences (lists of the form
\fC{args body ?ns?}\fR) are compiled and serialized as lambda\-bytecode literals at save time.
Classification belongs to that exact bytecode occurrence, not to equal bytes or a shared
Tcl_Obj, so an equal list used as ordinary data remains an ordinary literal.
On load, the compiled body, argument list, and optional namespace element are rehydrated
into a \fBProc\fR and registered in the \fBApplyShim\fR so that the first call to
\fBapply\fR does not trigger compilation. If type shimmer later evicts the lambdaExpr
internal representation, the ApplyShim transparently re\-installs it on the next
\fBapply\fR call.  Shim installation is idempotent and is retried by subsequent
loads after \fBapply\fR is renamed or recreated. Strict lambda source fields are empty;
include\-source retains exact body bytes for introspection without changing execution.

.SH SEMANTICS AND PERFORMANCE
.PP
Artifacts are portable across little\- and big\-endian hosts. The loader detects host byte order
once and decodes the little\-endian wire fields explicitly. Numeric jump tables use Tcl's
one\-word key representation on every host; a serialized numeric key that is not representable
by a 32\-bit evaluator is rejected instead of being truncated or treated as a string key.
.PP
The loader requires an exact major, minor, patch, and release\-type Tcl producer match. A complete
neutral-image preflight validates the policy, source fields, all section totals and tags,
instruction widths, literal/local/AuxData indices, direct and jump\-table targets, exception
boundaries, nested policy words, and trailing bytes before any interpreter-owned artifact
material is constructed. Reconstruction then uses one final packed ByteCode allocation and one
copy of each instruction stream, without compile\-environment staging arrays.
.PP
Loading evaluates the precompiled top\-level in the caller's current namespace with
\fBiPtr->scriptFile\fR set to the authored source path (if recorded), then installs
precompiled proc/method bodies and rehydrates lambda literals. The intent is to be
functionally indistinguishable from \fBsource\fR of the original script, with the benefit
of faster startup due to avoided parsing/compilation.

.SH PRECOMPILATION BOUNDARY
.PP
TBCX precompiles bodies and lambdas only when they are present in
statically identifiable literal positions (script\-body arguments to
commands like \fBforeach\fR, \fBwhile\fR, \fBtry\fR, \fBeval\fR, etc., or
lambda literals for \fBapply\fR). Strings assembled at runtime \(em for
example with \fBformat\fR, string interpolation, or \fBlist\fR construction
\(em still round\-trip correctly, but they remain ordinary data and compile
at execution time when Tcl evaluates them.

.SH OO SUPPORT
.PP
TBCX preserves normal TclOO class/object construction semantics by
executing the rewritten top\-level script, while substituting precompiled
bodies for recognized \fBoo::define\fR / \fBoo::objdefine\fR method forms.
Tested scenarios include class methods, self methods, per\-object methods,
private methods, inheritance (including diamond), mixins, filters, forwards,
abstract/singleton metaclasses, method rename/delete/export changes,
metaclasses with \fBself method\fR, and \fBnext\fR\-based constructor chaining.
.PP
Method visibility survives the round\-trip however it was expressed: definition
options (\fB\-export\fR, \fB\-unexport\fR, \fB\-private\fR), lexical
\fBprivate { ... }\fR blocks (TIP #500 true\-private), and same\-body
\fBexport\fR/\fBunexport\fR (and \fBself export\fR/\fBself unexport\fR) commands
are all folded into the per\-method scope byte at save time and re\-applied at load.
Class\-instance and per\-object methods sharing the same name coexist (distinct
origin keys), and per\-object methods \(em including true\-private ones \(em are
installed with precompiled bodies.  A method the saver left verbatim (non\-literal
name, arguments, or body) always keeps its authored body; precompiled records are
matched positionally, in definition order, and patch only stub\-sentinel bodies.
.PP
With \fB\-include\-source\fR, \fBinfo class definition\fR, \fBinfo class constructor\fR,
\fBinfo class destructor\fR, and \fBinfo object method\fR all return the authored body
text byte\-for\-byte, enabling introspection\-based clone and copy idioms to
work identically to the \fBsource\fR\-based baseline.

.SH MULTI\-INTERPRETER AND THREAD SUPPORT
.PP
TBCX follows Tcl's standard threading model: only the thread that created
an interpreter may call
.BR tbcx::save ,
.BR tbcx::load ,
.BR tbcx::dump ,
or
.B tbcx::gc
on that interpreter.  Multi\-thread support means multiple independent
interpreters, each used by its owning thread \(em not sharing one
interpreter across threads.  Calling a TBCX command from a non\-owning
thread returns
.B TCL_ERROR
with a diagnostic message.
.PP
The same artifact may be loaded independently into interpreters other than the
originating one. Moving an already-materialized embedded script object between
interpreters is not supported. A statically recognized literal
\fBinterp eval\fR crossing fails at save with
\fBTBCX EVAL CROSSINTERP UNSUPPORTED\fR. Strict and
include\-source artifacts never recompile retained text. Interpreter\-specific state (ApplyShim lambda registry,
load depth, OO shim state) is consolidated in a single per\-interpreter
record and cleaned up automatically when the interpreter is deleted.
Shared process\-wide state \(em the save\-side opcode dispatch table \(em is
initialized exactly once, under a mutex, at package initialization, so
concurrent \fBtbcx::save\fR calls from multiple threads are safe.
Debug builds (or builds compiled with \fB\-DTBCX_THREAD_CHECKS\fR)
additionally assert interpreter\-thread ownership inside internal helpers.

.SH LIMITS
.PP
Sanity caps exist for code size (64 MiB), literal/AuxData/exception counts (1M each),
string lengths (4 MiB), total serialized output (256 MB), serialization recursion depth (64),
total WriteLiteral calls (2M), and total WriteCompiledBlock calls (256K).
Every raw instruction and metadata reference is validated before ByteCode construction,
and unexplained trailing bytes are rejected.  Exceeding any limit produces an error.
.PP
Nested or reentrant \fBtbcx::load\fR calls are capped at depth 8 per
interpreter to prevent runaway recursive loading.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.PP
Representative messages include: "bad header", "incompatible Tcl version", "short read/write",
"unsupported AuxData kind", "input is neither an open channel nor a readable file",
"runaway serialization detected", "tbcx::save: unknown option \"\fI...\fR\"; expected -include-source",
"tbcx: called from non-owning thread",
and Tcl errors from top\-level evaluation.

.SH SECURITY
.PP
Loading executes code. Only load artifacts you trust.
.PP
Safe interpreters receive no \fBtbcx::*\fR commands by default; use
\fBinterp alias\fR or \fBinterp expose\fR from a parent interpreter to
grant selective access.

.SH SEE ALSO
source(n), TclOO(n), info(n), apply(n), interp(n), tclcompiler and tbcload (Tcl 8.x bytecode tools)

